  "id": "Compound::DB01154 - Gene::2556",
  "verbalization": "Thiamylal, a thiamine antagonist, is shown to bind to Gabra6 and Gabrq, both of which are involved in Gaba A receptor activation. This suggests that Thiamylal may interfere with the function of Gaba A receptors by binding to their associated subunits. The evidence indicates that Gabra3, a subunit of the Gaba A receptor, is a downstream target of these receptor activities. The integration of these pathways suggests that Thiamylal may modulate Gaba A receptor function through the interaction with Gabra6 and Gabrq, potentially affecting neurotransmitter transmission. The scores from the paths support a mechanistic link between Thiamylal and Gabra3, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The observed pathways highlight a potential role for Thiamylal in regulating Gaba A receptor activity, which could have implications for neurological function and disease.",
